When a discrete math problem feels unclear, write the relevant definition, construct a tiny example, and test the definition against that example. This habit is more reliable than trying to memorize many special cases.

Vertices and edges
A vertex is a node or object. An edge is a connection between vertices.
Example edge list:
V = {A, B, C, D}
E = {AB, AC, BC, CD}
This graph has four vertices and four edges.

Degree
In an undirected graph, the degree of a vertex is the number of edges incident to it.
For edges AB, AC, BC, CD:

degree of A is 2;
degree of B is 2;
degree of C is 3;
degree of D is 1.


Handshake idea
In an undirected graph, the sum of all degrees equals twice the number of edges, because each edge touches two endpoints.

